          "id": "c1",
          "value": {
            "language": "en",
            "text": "Warsaw University of Life Sciences (Szkoła Główna Gospodarstwa Wiejskiego, or SGGW) is a public agricultural university in Warsaw, Poland. Its institutional history traces to the School of Agronomy founded at Marymont on 23 September 1816. The university is based in Warsaw's Ursynów district, where its campus includes a historic palace area and a more recent part accommodating faculty buildings and student residences. The institution's published Warsaw address is Nowoursynowska 166, but the available evidence does not identify that address specifically as the main campus's address.\n\nThe early school developed through branches and institutes concerned with agronomy, veterinary medicine, rural economy and forestry. Under Russian imperial rule the institution was moved away from Warsaw, first to Puławy and later to Russia. Following Polish independence, it returned to Warsaw in 1918 and became the Major School of Rural Economy in 1919. Studies expanded to horticulture in 1921. The Second World War disrupted activity, which resumed in 1945; veterinary education joined through a faculty transfer from the University of Warsaw in 1952.\n\nThe university describes 13 faculties covering agricultural, economic, technical, humanities and life sciences, including veterinary medicine, forestry, food technology, biology and biotechnology. Its Ursynów campus includes a library, residences, sports facilities and a veterinary clinic. A Wikipedia infobox reports 15,254 students in December 2023; this is a dated observation, not an asserted current enrollment."
          },
          "verification": "supported"
